#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""omarchy-xray — Xray subscription manager for the Omarchy widget (v3).

Subcommands (run as your user; the manager refuses to run as root except for
the two explicit TUN install steps):
  import [-]         add a subscription (https:// URL) or servers (share links
                     such as vless:// or ss://, or Xray JSON: a config, a list
                     of configs or one outbound) to the "Manual" group. The
                     text is read from $OMARCHY_XRAY_SUB_URL or stdin when the
                     arg is "-", so secrets never appear in argv.
  subremove <key>    remove a subscription by index or host
  update [index]     re-fetch every subscription (or one), rebuild config
  select <key>       switch node: node key (k…), n<index>, "auto" or a name
                     substring; restarts the service when it is running
  mode <proxy|tun>   switch mode (TUN needs the one-time `tun-setup`)
  routing <preset>   global | <region>-direct (ru by kz uz tm cn ir tr ae sa eg pk vn mm)
  adblock <on|off>   block geosite:category-ads-all
  fragment <on|off> [packets length interval]
                     split the TLS ClientHello of TLS nodes (DPI; not REALITY/QUIC);
                     defaults: tlshello 100-200 10-20 (ranges "N" or "N-M")
  rule add <direct|proxy|block> <domain|ip/cidr|geosite:x|geoip:x>
  rule rm <index>    own routing rules, checked before the presets
  dns <preset>       DNS the TUN uses: cloudflare, google, quad9, adguard (DoH through
                     the tunnel) or system (the network's own DNS, outside the tunnel)
  fav <key>          star / unstar a node (starred nodes sort first)
  set <key> <value>  autofav on|off (Auto only among starred nodes), lan on|off
                     (socks/http for the local network, with a password),
                     mux on|off|<1-1024>, loglevel error|warning|info|debug
  set ua <text|default>  User-Agent for subscription downloads
  chain <key|off>    first hop: the selected node (or Auto) is dialed through this one
  share <key> [qr]   the node's share link (or its outbound JSON); qr: also a PNG path
  whoami             exit IP and country, asked through the tunnel
  speed [key]        download speed (Cloudflare, 25 MB max): the tunnel, or one node
                     on its own through a throwaway core
  scan               import from a QR code on screen (drag a region; grim, slurp, zbar)
  geo update         fresh geoip/geosite.dat (Loyalsoldier, sha256-checked) into
                     ~/.config/omarchy-xray/geo; the widget refreshes them (set geoupdate)
  set subupdate <auto|off|hours>  auto-update interval (auto: the provider's, or 24 h)
  set geoupdate <off|days>        how often the widget refreshes downloaded geo data (7)
  settings export    print the settings (no subscriptions, no secrets) as JSON
  settings import -  read such JSON from stdin
  dns custom <addr>  your own server through the tunnel: an IP, or https:// tls://
                     tcp:// quic:// h2c:// with an IP or a host name
  on | off | restart start / stop / restart the tunnel
  test [key…]        latency-test nodes (batched, parallel, bounded)
  status             machine-readable JSON for the widget (secrets redacted)
  logs [n]           last journal lines of the active service
  doctor             environment checks (xray, geo files, TUN, polkit, DNS)
  cleanup            before `omarchy plugin remove`: stop, undo tun-setup, drop
                     the user units and proxy settings (keeps the config)
  tun-setup          one-time TUN setup through polkit (pkexec) — see below
  tun-remove         undo tun-setup (through polkit)
  tun-install        [root] what tun-setup runs: writes the system unit, the
  tun-uninstall      [root] polkit rule and the networkd drop-in (and removes)
  tun-run            [user unit] tun2socks on xray0 + follow the default route
  proxy-sync         [user unit] apply or clear the proxy settings for the mode
  tun-unit <start|stop>  [user unit] start/stop the root unit, only if it is ours

Security model:
  - xray never gets a capability, in either mode: a systemd *user* unit runs it.
  - TUN mode: a root oneshot *system* unit `omarchy-xray-tun.service` runs only
    `ip`, `udevadm` and `resolvectl` — it creates the xray0 device owned by
    your uid, routes all traffic into it and points DNS at it; stopping it
    deletes the device. tun2socks (a user unit, no capabilities) moves the
    device's packets to xray's local socks port; xray's own connections are
    bound to the physical interface (unprivileged SO_BINDTODEVICE). No setcap,
    no sudoers; this script is never run as root after the one-time setup.
  - A polkit rule lets exactly your user start/stop/restart exactly that unit.
  - Subscription URLs are https-only, redirect-checked, byte-capped and
    strictly validated; node fields carry count/string bounds.
  - state.json holds secrets: private 0700 dir, 0600 no-follow atomic writes.
  - Child processes get an explicit environment allowlist and absolute paths.
"""
